XMA decoder fixes ported from Xenia Canary's XmaContextNew (BSD license),
which the ReXGlue SDK's XmaContext shares; v0.10.0 lacks them.

Work loop:
- ade7e610b "[XMA] Fix stall detection false positive in Work loop": leave the
  loop when Decode() makes no progress instead of spinning on one context,
  which held the worker and the guest threads waiting on it.
- 6bef1fbed "[XMA] Drain the current frame before ending the XMA work loop":
  the loop ended once both input buffers went invalid, stranding the rest of
  the current frame that a polling title waits for.

Output buffer validity:
- 505697f98 "refine output buffer invalidation" with the first half of
  09dbe2cd3 "do not invalidate output_buffer_valid prematurely": an empty
  output ring is ambiguous (nothing written, or full); invalidate only when it
  is full. Not taken from 09dbe2cd3: rewinding the write offset to the read
  offset when starved on input (an NFS stall detector), which discards output
  the title has not played yet.
- 7e98ae6de "[XMA] Invalidate output buffer if there is nothing to process":
  the consume-only path invalidates an empty output ring.

Packet parsing:
- 7015473a7 "Follow the sub-stream skip chain past frameless packets".
- aee0871dd "Count the frame whose header crosses the packet boundary".

Local fixes (not in Xenia Canary), for Broken Bond's cutscenes, which wait
forever for voice lines, and its streamed audio (docs/known-issues.md):
- A frame whose body continues into the other input buffer while that buffer
  is not valid yet set error 4 and held the current buffer. The decoder now
  keeps the frame's part, releases the buffer and completes the frame from the
  next buffer's first packet (DecodePendingSplitFrame; the frame decode moves
  to DecodeFrame), so no buffer is held and no frame is dropped.
- A frame whose 15-bit header is split into that buffer was dropped: the
  buffer was released without it and decoding went on at the first frame of
  the next buffer. Broken Bond submits one packet per buffer and refills a
  stream only while it is owed less than 2400 samples, counted from a
  per-packet sample table; a few frames lost this way left voices and music
  owed more than that forever (scenes waited for voice lines, music stopped).
  The frame is now kept and completed from the next buffer like a split body.
- Each decoded block is queued as it comes, as Xenia Canary does. The SDK's
  start-padding realignment (f24299f, for loop wraps) held every frame back
  until the next decode, so the last frame of a stream never came out and a
  title that counts a voice's samples waited for it forever. Flushing it once
  the input ran out and the output had played, with its last
  kDecoderStartPadding samples silent, also fired at every buffer boundary of a
  stream submitted one packet per buffer, whose output plays out before the
  next packet arrives: a gap in streamed voices and music at every boundary
  (crackles). Without the realignment a loop wrap may lose those samples again.
